[Music]  does god exist  whether you believe in god don't believe  in god or  aren't quite sure this question has been  posed by just about  every philosopher and every culture in  history  what made the universe what made  us is there someone  or something answering my prayers  throughout history there have been many  efforts to prove god's existence  not scientifically because as of yet  there is no scientific evidence for god  but  philosophically in this video i'd like  to go over the four  most important philosophical arguments  for the existence of god  the cosmological argument the  teleological argument  the ontological argument and the moral  argument and go over  some of their counter-arguments  the first and perhaps most popular is  the cosmological argument as the name  implies  the cosmological argument attempts to  prove god's existence  by looking to the existence of the  universe  or cosmos the argument originated in  ancient greece  with both plato and aristotle writing  versions of it  other philosophers like thomas aquinas  and dun scotus  two medieval catholic priests and  theologians  from italy and scotland respectively  also advocated for versions of it it's  important to note  these names are umbrella terms that  describe  a type of argument for the existence of  god there is no one  cosmological argument or one  teleological argument  but many different arguments espoused by  different people  that when simplified down are  essentially arguing the same thing  but i digress cosmological arguments are  often called  first cause arguments because they  describe a  first cause for the universe they are  arguments that suppose  since the universe exists there must  have been  a cause for its existence here's a  simple version one  everything that has a beginning as a  cause  two the universe had a beginning  three therefore the universe had a cause  and that cause is god this version has  some obvious holes  for one we don't know the universe had a  beginning  we think it probably did but it's not  inconceivable  that the universe is an eternal thing  also  just because it had a cause doesn't mean  the cause  was god it could have been a natural  phenomenon  here's a better though admittedly more  complicated version of the argument  one some contingent beings exist  two contingent beings require a cause  three there must exist a  necessary being as the ultimate cause  for therefore there exists  a necessary being i.e god  so there are a few terms we must explain  a contingent being is simply a thing  that's existence  is dependent on something else for  example you  your existence is contingent on your  parents meeting  if they never met you would not exist  okay so unnecessary being is something  that is not contingent on anything else  what this argument is saying is  essentially the same as the first  but in more let's say tight language it  still isn't airtight  again one could argue that just because  there is a necessary being that must  exist  doesn't mean god exists essentially the  cosmological argument postulates  that for the universe to exist at all  there must be a reason  this makes the argument very popular  because it plays on a natural human  instinct to find a reason for everything  the teleological argument is similar to  the cosmological argument  in that it argues for god's existence  based on the state of the universe  but unlike the cosmological argument it  focuses on the characteristics of the  universe  particularly its design rather than its  mere existence  because of this it's often called the  argument from  design it like the argument just  discussed  goes back to ancient greece and the  greatest propagators of it were the  stoic  philosophers other proponents for the  argument were william turner the  botanist  and william paley the 19th century  english philosopher  the argument posits that the universe is  so complicated and well  ordered that it must have been designed  here it is laid out 1. there exists  in nature a beneficial order to things  two beneficial order is best explained  by intelligent design  three therefore there was an intelligent  designer  i.e god a popular analogy for this  argument  is the watchmaker analogy created by  william paley  say you're walking down a path in the  forest and you happen across  a rock your assumption about the rock's  origins would be  that it came about through natural  causes the rock is simple  uniform and serves no obvious purpose  but say you walk a little further down  the path  and you see a watch on the ground your  assumption would not be the same as with  the raw  the watch was clearly made by someone  it is complex with many intricate parts  and all of those parts are working  together for a purpose  to tell time so a proponent for the  teleological argument  would say that as we look to the  universe  it is much more like the watch than the  rock  like the cosmological argument this  argument is not without its dissenters  first off the claim that the complex  nature of the universe could  only be made by design is not a clear  fact  as science has progressed we see that  many complex phenomena  happen through natural processes a good  example of this  is of course evolution that shows that  complex  things like animals can come to be  through the process of natural selection  in fact when darwin's theories first hit  the market  it was seen by many philosophers to put  the teleological argument to rest  there are modern philosophers who  disagree with his perspective  and argue that the theory could still  work while accepting the truth of  evolution  the teleological argument points to the  probability of god's existence  without trying to give a solid proof of  god outright  still the argument has stood the test of  time and remains popular  likely due to humankind's awe at the  complexity and majesty of our universe  the ontological argument is one of my  favorites  not because it's necessarily convincing  but more so  because of its complexity and outright  strangeness  it's an argument that many people can't  quite get their heads around at first  so hopefully i can explain it well  enough here  the ontological argument unlike the  cosmological or  teleological doesn't argue based on the  existence of the universe  or even anything in the universe but  instead  argues that god exists based solely on  the definition of god  and god's own characteristics it was  first put into words by saint anselm  the famed medieval theologian and bishop  there are  other ontological arguments but we use  his as our example  since it is the best known it goes  something like this  one god is the greatest being of which  it is possible to conceive  two god exists in the minds of humans  three a being that exists only in the  mind  and not in reality is lesser than a  being that exists both in the mind  and in reality four if god  only exists in the mind then god is not  the greatest possible thing that can be  conceived  five therefore god must by definition  exist both in the mind and in reality  so basically it argues that god by  definition  must exist as interesting as this  argument is  it has some major flaws one of these  flaws  was even pointed out by a contemporary  of saint anselm  the monk ganilo after reading the  argument ganilo  came up with a refutation he pointed out  that if you replace  god with say an island the argument  falls apart  say you imagine an island the greatest  island you can  think of it is big and beautiful  the trees all bear delicious fruit there  are no dangerous  animals that will hurt or kill you it is  perfect  of course this island doesn't exist what  ganilo  was pointing out is that existence is  not really a characteristic  that can be tied to greatness when  anselm heard of ganilo's refutation  he stated the argument only works for  necessary beings  not contingent ones the island is a  contingent thing  it can only exist if certain conditions  are met  but god is necessary and isn't  contingent on anything  this seems to many like a cop-out  saying that the argument works because  god is a unique case  still the argument has been very  influential  and it did something that the previous  two did not it gave  god specific characteristics  the last argument we will look at is the  moral argument  like the cosmological and teleological  argument it first appeared in ancient  greece  and has been propagated by the likes of  emmanuel kant  the famed philosopher and c.s lewis  the author of the chronicles of narnia  the argument  per its name argues that god is the  progenitor  of morality and so must exist  failing morals be arbitrary here it is  laid out  one unless there is a god there can be  no objective binding moral obligations  2 there are objective binding  moral obligations 3  therefore god must exist this argument  has  perhaps the most obvious of  counter-arguments  namely that there are no objective moral  obligations  this can be established via cultural  relativism  in which one might believe that morals  are a cultural artifact  and they change from society to society  or  individual relativism where one believes  that people  make up morals for themselves in reality  neither of these counter arguments are  as good as they seem on first viewing  first individual relativism is pretty  wrong on the face of it  as we can see that there is general  consensus on many moral issues  and second most people don't agree with  cultural relativism  it's a hotly debated subject also  cultural relativism doesn't disprove  objective morality  there's nothing to say that all cultures  are morally correct  one culture's morals could simply be  objectively wrong  a much more popular counter to the moral  argument  is that objective morality doesn't  necessarily have to come from  god it could be inherent within humans  due to some natural occurrence  like evolution the moral argument backs  up into the question of ethics  which by itself is an entire branch of  philosophy  one that has been discussed and argued  about for literally thousands of years  because of this it is in my opinion one  of the weaker  arguments we've looked at still the  moral argument does a good job of  employing the moral consciousness  that is near universal within human  beings  hopefully that has given you a lot to  think about by the way  this video is not meant as an  endorsement of any of these arguments  nor  as a refutation of them it is also not  an endorsement of any belief in god nor  non-belief in god please be kind in the  comments  and thanks for watching  hello i really appreciate you checking  out my video  if you want to see more like this please  subscribe like the video if you liked it  leave a comment letting me know what you  think and check out some of my other  videos if you want to learn more  bye  you

